  "gamePlayAgain": "Play again",
  "lesson1Card1Title": "What is a variable?",
  "@lesson1Card1Speech": {
    "description": "Lesson text. Very simple English: 8-10 words per sentence, one idea each. Words in `backticks` are code: keep them in English."
  },
  "lesson1Card1Speech": "Beep boop! I am Cog. Robots need to remember things. I remember my battery in a variable. A variable is like a box. The box has a name on it. The thing inside is the value.",
  "lesson1Card1Tip": "A variable is a box with a name. It holds a value.",
  "lesson1Card2Title": "Notes in code",
  "lesson1Card2Speech": "You can write notes in your code. A note starts with `#`. We call it a comment. A comment is a note for people. Python skips it completely. It does nothing to the code. It only helps people read the code.",
  "lesson1Card2Tip": "Python skips everything after `#` on that line.",
  "lesson1Card3Title": "Making a variable",
  "lesson1Card3Speech": "Let's make a variable! First, write the name. Next, write `=`. Last, write the value. The `=` means \"put this in the box.\"",
  "lesson1Card3Tip": "In Python, `=` does not mean \"equal.\" It means \"put this value in the box.\"",
  "lesson1Card4Title": "Kinds of values",
  "lesson1Card4Speech": "A box can hold different kinds of values. It can hold a number, like `5`. It can hold a word in quotes, like `\"Cog\"`. It can hold `True` or `False`. They mean yes or no.",
  "lesson1Card4Tip": "Words need quotes. Numbers do not. `True` and `False` start with a big letter.",
  "lesson1Card5Title": "Changing a variable",
  "lesson1Card5Speech": "I walked a lot. My battery went down! Let's change it. Python reads code from top to bottom. First, the box gets 100. Then, the box gets 80. The 100 is gone. Now the box has 80.",
  "lesson1Card5Tip": "A box keeps only its newest value.",
  "lesson1Card6Title": "Naming rules",
  "lesson1Card6Speech": "Every box needs a good name. Follow my rules:\n• No spaces. Use `_` instead.\n• Do not start with a number.\n• Big and small letters are different. `speed` and `Speed` are two boxes!\n• Pick a name that makes sense.",
  "lesson1Card6Tip": "Good names help people read your code.",
  "@lesson1NoteBattery100": {
    "description": "Arrow note shown after a code line (\"← note\"). Plain, short words. Variable names like battery stay in English."
  },
  "lesson1NoteBattery100": "battery is now 100",
  "lesson1NoteSpeed5": "speed is now the number 5",
  "lesson1NoteRobotName": "robot_name is now the word Cog",
  "lesson1NoteIsMoving": "is_moving is now True (yes)",
  "lesson1NoteBattery80": "battery is now 80. The 100 is gone.",
  "lesson1NoteGoodUnderscore": "Good! _ takes the place of the space",
  "lesson1NoteBadSpace": "No spaces allowed",
  "lesson1NoteGoodNumberEnd": "OK! The number is at the end",
  "lesson1NoteBadNumberStart": "Can't start with a number",
  "lesson1NoteSpeedSmall": "speed is one box",
  "lesson1NoteSpeedBig": "Speed is a different box!",
  "lesson1NoteGoodClear": "Clear name",
  "lesson1NoteBadUnclear": "What is b? Not clear.",
